---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro-expressive-code/components';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import HelpFooter from '../components/HelpFooter.astro';
import BottomNav from '../components/BottomNav.astro';

const LESSONS_URL = 'https://github.com/DominikPieper/atelier-ui/blob/main/tasks/lessons.md';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Runbook"
  description="Operational notes for running the Atelier setup day to day — how the Figma Desktop Bridge plugin updates, how stale server instances happen, and how to clear them."
  currentPath="/runbook"
>
  <div class="docs-inline-page">

    <PageHero kind="howto" title="Runbook" ledeMax="680px">
      <a href="/figma-token" class="docs-link-primary">/figma-token</a> gets the Desktop Bridge
      running once. <a href="/troubleshooting" class="docs-link-primary">/troubleshooting</a> fixes
      one specific symptom. This page is neither — it collects what stays true about running the
      setup <em>afterward</em>: what refreshes itself, what doesn't, and the housekeeping that keeps
      the Bridge usable across a long session. It grows by adding entries, not by redesigning them.
    </PageHero>

    <!-- In this runbook — an index, and the extension point: a third entry
         gets one <li> here and one <section id="…"> below. -->
    <nav aria-label="Runbook entries" style="margin: 0 0 2.25rem; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised); border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border); border-radius: var(--ui-radius-md)">
      <div style="font-size: var(--ui-font-size-xs); font-weight: 700; color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; margin-bottom: 0.6rem">In this runbook</div>
      <ol style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--ui-font-size-md)">
        <li><a href="#plugin-update" class="docs-link-primary">Updating the Figma Desktop Bridge plugin</a></li>
        <li><a href="#stale-instances" class="docs-link-primary">Stale server instances competing for the Bridge</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <!-- Entry 1 -->
    <section id="plugin-update" style="margin-bottom: 2.75rem">
      <h2 class="docs-section-title">Updating the Figma Desktop Bridge plugin</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; line-height: 1.65">
        "The plugin" is really two things, and only one of them updates itself. Confusing the two
        is the entire failure mode this entry exists to prevent.
      </p>

      <Callout tone="tip" title="Refreshes itself, on every server start">
        <p>
          Every time a <code>figma-console-mcp</code> server starts, its <code>setupStablePluginDir()</code>
          function (in the package's <code>dist/local.js</code>) copies <code>manifest.json</code>,
          <code>code.js</code>, and <code>ui.html</code> out of the npx cache into
          <code>~/.figma-console-mcp/plugin/</code> and rewrites <code>.version</code> there —
          unconditionally, not only when something changed. In the package's own words, that stable
          directory exists as "a permanent, predictable path to import from instead of the volatile
          npx cache path that changes between updates."
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <Callout tone="warning" title="Does not refresh itself: Figma Desktop's already-open plugin">
        <p>
          Figma only reads a plugin's <code>code.js</code> and <code>ui.html</code> when you run
          it — from <strong>Plugins → Development</strong>, or automatically if
          <strong>Hot Reloading Plugin</strong> is on and Figma notices <code>manifest.json</code>'s
          file-modification time changed. Until one of those happens, whatever Figma loaded last
          keeps running — even once the files under <code>~/.figma-console-mcp/plugin/</code> are
          already current.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Check which version you have</h3>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">Three different signals answer "which version," and each answers a different question:</p>
      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: var(--ui-font-size-md); color: var(--ui-color-text); line-height: 1.6">
        <div>
          <code>.version</code> — the npm package version <code>setupStablePluginDir()</code> last
          wrote to <code>~/.figma-console-mcp/plugin/.version</code>, refreshed on every server
          start. Read it with <code>cat ~/.figma-console-mcp/plugin/.version</code>.
        </div>
        <div>
          <code>figma_get_status</code>'s <code>bundledPluginVersion</code> — parsed from the
          <code>PLUGIN_VERSION</code> constant inside the <code>code.js</code> <em>this running
          server ships</em>. The package's own source describes it as "what a manifest re-import
          installs" and deliberately not the npm package version, since a server-only release can
          bump one without touching the plugin bundle. A mismatch against <code>.version</code> is
          not drift — they are two different counters by design.
        </div>
        <div>
          <code>figma_get_status</code>'s per-file <code>pluginVersion</code> (with
          <code>pluginUpdateAvailable</code>) — the version the plugin panel <em>currently open
          inside Figma Desktop</em> reports about itself, live, over the WebSocket. This is the only
          one of the three that tells you what's actually loaded right now.
          <code>pluginUpdateAvailable: true</code> means it differs from
          <code>bundledPluginVersion</code> — that's the real "go re-run or re-import" signal, not a
          disagreement with <code>.version</code>.
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        For the version this repo currently pins, read the <code>figma-console</code> entry's
        <code>args</code> in <code>.mcp.json</code> rather than trusting a number written here — the
        pin moves independently of this page.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Update steps</h3>
      <div class="docs-step-list">
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">01</span>
          <div>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              <strong>Code or UI changed, <code>manifest.json</code> did not:</strong> just close
              and re-run the plugin — <strong>Plugins → Development → Figma Desktop Bridge</strong>
              again, or restart Figma Desktop. Re-running re-reads <code>code.js</code> and
              <code>ui.html</code> from disk; no re-import needed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">02</span>
          <div>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              <strong><code>manifest.json</code> itself changed</strong> (a wider port range, a new
              permission, anything else in that file): re-import —
              <strong>Plugins → Development → Import plugin from manifest…</strong> and pick the same
              <code>~/.figma-console-mcp/plugin/manifest.json</code> again. See the callout below for
              why this is the safe default rather than a confirmed requirement.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-step-row">
          <span class="docs-step-badge">03</span>
          <div>
            <p class="docs-step-desc">
              Verify: call <code>figma_get_status</code> and check the connected file's
              <code>pluginUpdateAvailable</code> — absent or <code>false</code> means Figma is now
              running what's on disk.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Callout tone="note" title="What Figma's own docs settle — and what they don't">
        <p>
          Figma's plugin docs confirm that re-running a plugin re-reads its code: the
          <a href="https://developers.figma.com/docs/plugins/plugin-quickstart-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="docs-link-primary">quickstart guide</a>
          describes <strong>Hot Reloading Plugin</strong> restarting the plugin "with the latest
          changes" as you rebuild, and requiring a manual restart otherwise — either way, a restart
          re-reads <code>code.js</code>/<code>ui.html</code> from disk.
        </p>
        <p>
          What neither that guide, the
          <a href="https://developers.figma.com/docs/plugins/manifest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="docs-link-primary">manifest reference</a>,
          nor the <a href="https://developers.figma.com/docs/plugins/how-plugins-run/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="docs-link-primary">how-plugins-run</a>
          page says is whether a <code>manifest.json</code> change alone — like this plugin's port
          range — is picked up by that same restart, or needs a full re-import. Step 02 above
          re-imports whenever <code>manifest.json</code> changed and just re-runs otherwise: the
          procedure that is safe under either reading, at the cost of a few extra seconds.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); font-size: 0.8rem; margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.6">
        You may also see <code>ui-full.html</code> next to <code>ui.html</code> in that directory.
        It isn't part of <code>setupStablePluginDir()</code>'s copy list (<code>manifest.json</code>,
        <code>code.js</code>, <code>ui.html</code> only), and <code>manifest.json</code>'s
        <code>ui</code> field points at <code>ui.html</code>, not it — a leftover from an older
        build, inert either way.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Entry 2 -->
    <section id="stale-instances" style="margin-bottom: 1rem">
      <h2 class="docs-section-title">Stale server instances competing for the Bridge</h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Every launch of <code>figma-console-mcp</code> — a Claude Code session, a subagent, a
        background eval loop — starts its own server process. Each one opens a WebSocket and binds
        a port in <code>9223–9232</code>, falling back down the range if its preferred port is
        already taken. But Figma Desktop runs exactly one Desktop Bridge plugin instance, and it
        connects to exactly one of those servers. Every other running process is orphaned: alive,
        listening, reported under <code>figma_get_status</code>'s <code>otherInstances</code> — and
        never getting the plugin's attention.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Why it matters</h3>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        <a href={LESSONS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="docs-link-primary">tasks/lessons.md</a>
        has the record of this actually costing a session (2026-09-08): a background
        trigger-optimisation loop and eight Figma-dependent eval agents ran in the same minute, and
        "within a minute the parent session's <code>figma-console</code> MCP disconnected and its
        125 tools vanished" as roughly two dozen orphaned servers competed for the one Desktop
        Bridge connection. All eight agents stopped, every partial output was discarded, and the
        session had to be reconnected by hand. The lesson's own rule: background or bulk Figma work
        runs <strong>alone</strong>, after every Figma-dependent task in the session is finished.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        As of this writing, <code>figma_get_status</code> lists seven such orphaned instances on
        ports 9224–9230 in this environment, left over from parallel eval runs. That count is a
        snapshot, not a fact this page should keep current — call <code>figma_get_status</code> for
        the live number.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Cleanup</h3>
      <Code code={`pkill -f figma-console-mcp`} lang="shell" />
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        This is the same command the package's own diagnostics suggest: <code>figma_get_status</code>'s
        recovery steps for an unconnected bridge include "Kill stale processes if needed:
        <code>pkill -f figma-console-mcp</code>." It matches by process name, so it also stops a
        legitimate server your current session depends on — safe to run, since your AI client starts
        a fresh one automatically on its next tool call, but worth a beat if you're mid-task rather
        than cleaning up between them.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Prevention</h3>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Run background or bulk Figma-dependent work (eval loops, trigger-optimisation runs, anything
        that spawns several headless sessions) alone — after every other Figma- or
        Claude-Design-dependent task in the session has finished — and, where the runner supports
        it, with an MCP config that gives those headless sessions no servers of their own at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; background: var(--ui-color-primary-light); border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border); border-radius: var(--ui-radius-md)">
      <p style="margin: 0; font-size: var(--ui-font-size-md); color: var(--ui-color-text); line-height: 1.6">
        First-time Figma setup lives at <a href="/figma-token" class="docs-link-primary">/figma-token</a>;
        a specific symptom you're chasing right now probably has a faster answer at
        <a href="/troubleshooting" class="docs-link-primary">/troubleshooting</a>. This page is for
        the operational facts in between.
      </p>
    </div>

    <HelpFooter page="runbook" />

    <BottomNav items={[
      { to: '/figma-token', label: 'Setup', title: 'Figma access', icon: 'palette' },
      { to: '/troubleshooting', label: 'Fix', title: 'Troubleshooting', icon: 'help' },
      { to: '/mcp', label: 'Reference', title: 'MCP playground', icon: 'smart_toy' },
    ]} />

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
